<BaseLayout current="about" title="About · Pradyut Kumar">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I grew up between <em>[hometown]</em> and caffeinated thought experiments. My path into
    mathematical biology was a slow drift rather than a lightning strike — an engineering
    degree that taught me to take systems apart, a biology degree that taught me which
    systems are worth taking apart, and a few professors along the way who insisted the
    two could live in the same sentence.
  </p>

  <h2>Currently</h2>
  <p>
    PhD candidate at Purdue, working on transmission dynamics, drug resistance, and the
    statistics of very small numbers of infections. I try to write code that I'll still
    understand three months from now. It doesn't always work.
  </p>

  <h2>Previously</h2>
  <p>
    Dual degree at BITS Pilani (M.Sc. Biological Sciences · B.E. Electronics &amp;
    Instrumentation). Earlier projects touched signal processing, microscopy image
    analysis, and — briefly — the world's worst attempt at a hardware startup.
  </p>

  <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  <p>
    Outside research I sketch, read too many things at once, and am slowly learning to
    make better coffee than my apartment deserves.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
